TASK: Fix Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Real-Time Synchronization Across SmartWave PMS
The role-based access system in my SmartWave PMS is not reflecting permission changes live. When a Hotel Admin changes a staff member's role, module access, or individual permissions, the changes do not immediately reflect in the affected user's active session.
Investigate and fix this issue across the existing application.
1. Strict Preservation Requirements
Do not rewrite, restructure, scatter, or replace the existing application architecture.
Do not change existing business logic, financial logic, reservation logic, or unrelated modules.
Do not introduce Firestore indexes.
Do not create a separate permission system or duplicate existing RBAC logic.
Reuse the existing Firestore collections, user documents, role definitions, permission structures, and authorization functions.
Make only the changes necessary to correct real-time RBAC synchronization.
Preserve existing SuperAdmin, Hotel Admin, and staff access boundaries.
2. Investigate the Actual Cause
Before implementing anything, inspect the existing authentication, staff management, role assignment, permission management, Firestore data structures, and access-control logic.
Identify why permission changes are not reflected immediately.
Investigate specifically:
Whether the application loads user roles and permissions only once during login.
Whether role and permission data are stored in React state or context without being refreshed.
Whether the application uses getDoc() or getDocs() for permissions that should remain synchronized.
Whether the existing onSnapshot() listener is missing, incorrectly configured, or attached to the wrong document or collection.
Whether permission updates are saved to a different Firestore document than the one the active session reads.
Whether the application maintains duplicate or inconsistent permission state.
Whether browser sessions continue using stale cached permissions.
Whether different modules implement their own permission checks instead of using the existing centralized authorization logic.
Whether Firestore security rules or frontend authorization logic prevent updated permissions from being recognized.
Do not assume the cause. Verify it from the existing implementation.
3. Implement Reliable Real-Time Permission Synchronization
Use the existing Firestore structure and authorization architecture.
Where appropriate, establish or correct real-time listeners using Firestore onSnapshot() for the authenticated user's relevant role and permission documents.
The active user's permission state should automatically update when:
A Hotel Admin changes the user's role.
A Hotel Admin grants or removes a module permission.
A Hotel Admin changes individual action permissions.
A user's access is suspended or revoked.
A user's assigned department or staff group changes.
Existing role definitions or permission assignments are updated, where the current architecture supports this.
The application must not require a page refresh, logout, or login to recognize these changes.
Ensure that permission updates propagate to all active sessions belonging to the affected user, including sessions open in different browser tabs or devices.
4. Update Access Across All Existing Modules
Ensure updated permissions immediately affect the existing PMS modules, including:
Dashboard
Front Desk
Reservations
Room Management
Guest Accounts and Folios
Payments and Checkout
Food and Beverage
Housekeeping
Maintenance
Laundry
Kitchen
Storekeeping and Inventory
Corporate Accounts
Financial Reports
Staff Management
Settings and other existing modules
Do not grant access to an entire module when the user has permission for only a specific action.
For example, if a staff member is granted permission to view a module but not create, edit, delete, approve, or settle transactions, the application must continue enforcing those individual restrictions.
5. Immediate Revocation and Security
Real-time permission synchronization must improve security, not merely update the interface.
When access is revoked:
Remove the restricted module or action from the user's active interface immediately.
Prevent the user from continuing to perform restricted actions through an already-open screen.
Revalidate authorization before sensitive Firestore writes and protected operations.
Do not rely solely on hiding buttons or menu items.
Preserve existing Firestore security rules and backend authorization protections.
Do not allow staff to modify their own roles or permissions unless the existing authorization system explicitly permits it.
Ensure Hotel Admin permissions remain limited to their own hotel.
Preserve SuperAdmin's existing cross-hotel authorization boundaries.
If a user is suspended or their account is disabled, apply the existing account-status and access-revocation mechanisms immediately.
6. Listener and State Management
Implement the solution carefully:
Avoid duplicate onSnapshot() subscriptions.
Clean up listeners when users log out, switch accounts, or components unmount.
Prevent stale listeners from updating the wrong user's session.
Avoid unnecessary repeated Firestore reads and excessive rerenders.
Prevent permission state from being overwritten by outdated asynchronous requests.
Preserve loading and error handling.
Do not introduce infinite listener loops.
Ensure the application behaves correctly when connectivity is temporarily lost and restored.
Do not blindly replace every Firestore read with onSnapshot(). Use real-time listeners only where continuous synchronization is required.
7. Firestore Security Rules
Inspect the existing Firestore security rules to determine whether they correctly support the current RBAC structure.
Do not overwrite the existing rules.
If a rule modification is genuinely necessary, append or minimally modify only the relevant permission-related conditions while preserving every existing rule and authorization boundary.
Do not weaken security to make the application appear functional.
8. Testing and Verification
Test the complete permission lifecycle:
A staff member logs in with existing permissions.
Hotel Admin removes one permission.
The staff member's active session immediately loses that permission.
Hotel Admin grants a permission.
The staff member's active session immediately receives that permission.
Hotel Admin changes the user's role.
The active session reflects the updated role and corresponding permissions.
Hotel Admin suspends the user.
The affected user loses access immediately.
Multiple active sessions remain synchronized.
Users from other hotels are not affected.
Existing SuperAdmin and Hotel Admin access remains correct.
9. Final Requirement
Fix the actual underlying problem rather than adding a temporary refresh button or requiring users to log in again.
After implementation, provide a concise report explaining:
The actual root cause.
The existing files or components that were modified.
How real-time permission synchronization now works.
How immediate permission revocation is enforced.
Any remaining limitations.
Important: Do not claim that RBAC is fully fixed unless the actual permission changes have been tested across active sessions and relevant modules.
